---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { SRC_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Two modalities — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'The verifiable path and the emergent QPU seam, separated by a cargo feature rather than by convention. What the qpu feature contains, and what it deliberately does not.';

const seam = `use deep_causality_quantum::{GateOp, QuantumCircuit, SimQpu, qpu_effect};

// A circuit is inert data: no amplitudes, no function pointers.
let circuit = QuantumCircuit::new(
    2,
    vec![GateOp::H(0), GateOp::Cnot { control: 0, target: 1 }],
    vec![0, 1],
)?;

// The sampler is a generic bound, never a trait object.
let qpu = SimQpu::new(0xC0FFEE);
let effect = qpu_effect(&qpu, &circuit, 1024);`;

const bridge = `// One measured qubit as a Bernoulli, at its measured frequency.
let bit: Uncertain<bool> = shots_to_qubit_bernoulli(&hist, 0)?;

// An observable as a normal, from the count entries rather than
// one materialized sample per shot.
let obs: Uncertain<f64> = shots_to_observable(&hist, |outcome| {
    if outcome.count_ones() % 2 == 0 { 1.0 } else { -1.0 }
})?;`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/modalities/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Modality"
    title="Verifiable and emergent"
    lede="Two things get called quantum in this workspace. One is simulated and proved; the other is a boundary to hardware. A cargo feature keeps them apart."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/types/qpu`}
    sourceLabel="src/types/qpu"
  >
    <h2>The split</h2>

    <p>
      The verifiable modality is the default build. Its operators are
      deterministic simulated Choi–Jamiołkowski matrices, carried as freeze-time
      decoration and checked at the freeze boundary. Every LEAN theorem in the
      workspace's quantum tree attaches to this path.
    </p>

    <p>
      The emergent modality is a physical QPU call lifted into the causal monad
      as a typed effect. It is not a LEAN target by nature; a claim about
      hardware is not the kind of thing a proof assistant settles. Its evidence
      is tests and provenance.
    </p>

    <p>
      Separating them by a feature rather than by documentation means the
      separation is checkable. A default build contains no sampler at all, so a
      statement about the verifiable path cannot quietly acquire a hardware
      caveat.
    </p>

    <h2>What the qpu feature contains</h2>

    <p>
      A circuit is inert data. <code>GateOp</code> has eight variants,
      <code>H</code>, <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, <code>Z</code>,
      <code>S</code>, <code>T</code>, <code>Cnot</code> and <code>Cz</code>,
      each carrying qubit indices and nothing else. <code>QuantumCircuit</code>
      holds a register width, an ordered program and a measurement list, and it
      is <code>Clone</code>, <code>Debug</code> and <code>PartialEq</code>.
      Nothing in it references an amplitude or a function pointer, which is what
      lets an in-process simulator and a future cloud adapter accept the same
      value.
    </p>

    <p>
      The constructor rejects a zero-qubit register, a gate referencing a qubit
      at or beyond the register width, a two-qubit gate with coincident control
      and target, a measurement of an out-of-range qubit, and a qubit measured
      more than once. That last one matters more than it looks: a repeated
      measurement both inflates the outcome table beyond the register and makes
      the bit order ambiguous.
    </p>

    <Code code={seam} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <h2>The sampler seam</h2>

    <p>
      <code>QpuSampler</code> has two associated types and two methods.
      <code>sample</code> runs a circuit for a number of shots and returns
      <code>Self::Shots</code>; <code>calibration</code> returns device
      metadata. The trait is used only as a generic bound, never as a trait
      object, in line with the workspace's static-dispatch rule.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>Self::Shots</code> is bounded by <code>ShotHistogram</code>, and
      that bound is where the design does its work. The trait exposes a total, a
      bit width, a count for one outcome, and the non-zero entries. It exposes
      no amplitudes and has no method that could. The coherence boundary is
      therefore pinned at the type level rather than by a convention someone
      could forget.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>SimQpu</code> is the hardware-free default: a deterministic dense
      state-vector simulator seeded by a <code>u64</code>, using splitmix64 for
      its draws, so the same seed reproduces the same histogram exactly. It caps
      at 24 qubits, which is 2²⁴ amplitudes, and refuses a wider circuit with
      <code>DimensionMismatch</code>. Its gate dispatch is exhaustive over
      <code>GateOp</code> with no catch-all arm, so adding a gate variant breaks
      the build rather than silently mis-applying it.
    </p>

    <p>
      No concrete vendor adapter ships with the crate. The seam is a boundary,
      not a driver.
    </p>

    <h2>Shots into uncertainty</h2>

    <p>
      Measurement statistics leave the seam as
      <code>deep_causality_uncertain</code> values, so no new notion of an
      uncertain quantity is introduced.
    </p>

    <Code code={bridge} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>shots_to_qubit_bernoulli</code> gives one measured qubit as a
      Bernoulli whose success probability is the measured frequency of 1 on that
      bit, counting LSB-first in measurement order.
      <code>shots_to_observable</code> maps each outcome to a real value and
      summarizes the empirical mean and the unbiased sample variance as a
      normal. It accumulates directly from the count entries, so its cost is the
      number of distinct outcomes rather than the number of shots. Both refuse
      an empty histogram.
    </p>

    <h2>The lift</h2>

    <p>
      <code>qpu_effect</code> puts a sampler call into the arity-5 causal monad
      and spreads the result across the five channels. The shot histogram rides
      the value channel; the requested parameters, as
      <code>QpuParams</code>, ride the state channel; the device calibration
      rides the context channel; the provenance string rides the log channel.
      A job failure rides the error channel with the value absent.
    </p>

    <p>
      Enabling <code>qpu</code> adds <code>deep_causality_uncertain</code> as a
      dependency. It adds nothing network-facing and nothing async.
    </p>
  </DocPage>

  <div class="wide-wrap">
    <ApiTable layer="qpu" title="The qpu surface" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wide-wrap {
    max-width: var(--w-panel);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-9);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .wide-wrap { padding: 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
</style>
